<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>7.11.4: Using the Vertex and Axis of Symmetry of Quadratics</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01930</md:content-id>
    <md:title>7.11.4: Using the Vertex and Axis of Symmetry of Quadratics</md:title>
    <md:uuid>5201447f-8e34-47de-b0a0-55c039441731</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section id="sect-00001">
      <title>Activity</title>
      <para id="para-00001">While answering the questions below, plot each point on a coordinate plane with grid paper by hand. Do not use technology.</para>
      <para id="para-00002">For questions 1 – 4, use the the function \(f(x)=(x-2)(x+4).\)</para>
      <!--Q#1-->
      <exercise>
        <problem id="prob-00001">
          <para id="para-00003"> Find the \(x\)-coordinates of the \(x\)-intercepts of \(f(x)\).</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00001">
          <para id="para-00004"/>
          <para id="para-00005">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00006">\(x=-4\) and \(x =2\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Q#2-->
      <exercise>
        <problem id="prob-00002">
          <para id="para-00007"> Find the coordinate of the vertex.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00002">
          <para id="para-00008"/>
          <para id="para-00009">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00010">\((-1, -5)\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Q#3-->
      <example id="exam-00001">
        <exercise>
          <problem id="prob-00003">
            <para id="para-00011"> On your graph, sketch a quadratic through the key features on your graph and draw a vertical line through the vertex using a different color. </para>
            <para id="para-00012">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00003">
            <para id="para-00013">Compare your answer:</para>
            <para id="para-00014">
              <figure id="fig-00001">
                <media alt="Insert Alt Text">
                  <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_13_09_01.jpg"/>
                </media>
              </figure>
           </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </example>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Q#4-->
      <exercise>
        <problem id="prob-00004">
          <para id="para-00015"> What is the equation of the vertical line that goes through the vertex?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00004">
          <para id="para-00016"/>
          <para id="para-00017">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00018">\(x=-1\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Q#5-->
      <exercise>
        <problem id="prob-00005">
          <para id="para-00019"> What does the vertical line that goes through the vertex do to the parabola?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00005">
          <para id="para-00020"/>
          <para id="para-00021">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00022">Your answer may vary, but here is a sample. The vertical line \(x= -1\) cuts the parabola in half and creates two symmetrical parts.</para>
          <para id="para-00023">This imaginary vertical line that goes through the vertex and cuts the parabola in half is called the <term class="glossary-tooltip" id="term-00001">axis of symmetry</term>.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Q#6-->
      <example id="exam-00002">
        <exercise>
          <problem id="prob-00006">
            <para id="para-00024"> On your graph, label your \(x\)-intercepts, vertex, and axis of symmetry. Identify your vertex as a maximum or minimum.</para>
            <para id="para-00025">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00006">
            <para id="para-00026">Compare your answer:</para>
            <para id="para-00027">
              <figure id="fig-00002">
                <media alt="Insert Alt Text">
                  <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_13_09_02.jpg"/>
                </media>
              </figure>
           </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </example>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Q#7-->
      <exercise>
        <problem id="prob-00007">
          <para id="para-00028"> Given a vertex of \((2, -4)\) and a point \((1, 3)\), what is another point on the quadratic?</para>
          <para id="para-00029">(Hint: It is helpful to draw a picture and use the axis of symmetry).</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00007">
          <para id="para-00030"/>
          <para id="para-00031">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00032">Your answer may vary, but here is a sample.</para>
          <para id="para-00033">
            <figure id="fig-00003">
              <media alt="Insert Alt Text">
                <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_13_09_03.jpg"/>
              </media>
            </figure>
         </para>
          <para id="para-00034">Since \((-2, 4)\) is the vertex, then \(x=-2\) is the axis of symmetry. If \((1, 3)\) is 3 units away from the axis of symmetry, there is another point 3 units to the left of the axis of symmetry. This is the point \((-5, 3)\).</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
    </section>

    <note class="self-check">
      <para> What is the equation of the axis of symmetry for \(f(x)=(x+1)(x-3)\)?</para>
    </note>

    <section>
      <title>Axis of Symmetry of a Quadratic </title>
      <para>In the graph of a quadratic function, the axis of symmetry is an imaginary line that cuts the parabola in half to create two symmetrical sides. It goes through the vertex of the parabola so the equation of the axis of symmetry is the same as the \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex.</para>
      <para>
        <figure>
          <media alt="Insert Alt Text">
            <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_13_11_01.jpg"/>
          </media>
        </figure>
        <emphasis id="docs-internal-guid-1a59f64e-7fff-f686-b37e-3c95d10108a1"/>
     </para>
      <para>Notice that in Figure 1, the vertex is at \((-2, -2)\) and the axis of symmetry is at \(x= -2\).</para>
      <para>In Figure 2, the vertex is at \((2, 7)\) and the axis of symmetry is at \(x =2\).</para>
      <para>
       <emphasis>Example</emphasis>
     </para>
      <para>Find the vertex and axis of symmetry for \(f(x)=(x+2)(x-6)\).</para>
      <para><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> -  Find the \(x\)-intercepts.
  Set each factor equal to 0.
  \(x+2=0\)
  \(x=-2\)
  \(x-6=0\)
  \(x=6\)</para>
      <para><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Find the \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex.
  The vertex is halfway between the two \(x\)-intercepts.
  \(x=6+(-2)^2=2\)
  \(x = \frac{6+(-2)}{2} =2\)</para>
      <para><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Find the \(y\)-coordinate of the vertex.
  Substitute the \(x\)-coordinate into \(f(x)\).
  \(f(2)=(2+2)(2-6)=(4)(-4)=-6\)
  The vertex is at \((2, -16)\).</para>
      <para><emphasis>Step 4</emphasis> - Find the axis of symmetry.
  The axis of symmetry is the equation where \(x\) equals the \(x\)-coordinate of the function.
  \(x= 2\)</para>
      <note class="try look-at-me"/>
  <title>Try It: Axis of Symmetry of a Quadratic</title>
      <para>For questions 1 - 2, use the equation of the quadratic, \(f(x)=(x-4)(x-10)\).</para>
      <!--Q#1-->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>What is the vertex of the quadratic?</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>\((7, -9)\)</para>
          <para><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Find the \(x\)-intercepts.
      Set each factor equal to 0.
      \(x-4=0\)
      \(x=4\)
      \(x-10=0\)
      \(x=10\)</para>
          <para><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Find the \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex.
      The vertex is halfway between the two \(x\)-intercepts.
      \(x=\frac{4+10}{2}=7\)</para>
          <para><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Find the \(y\)-coordinate of the vertex.
      Substitute the \(x\)-coordinate into \(f(x)\).
      \(f(7)=(7-4)(7-10)=(3)(-3)=-9\)
      The vertex is at \((7, -9)\).</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Q#2-->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>What is the axis of symmetry of the quadratic?</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>\(x=7\)</para>
          <para>The axis of symmetry is the equation where \(x\) equals the \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex.
      \(x= 7\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
    </section>
  </content>
